[[Category:Pages without translation templates]] [[Category:Pages with valid en interwikis]] [[Category:pages with valid en interwikis 2]] [[Category:qq]][[sn:Sōja shrine]][[Category:qq]] <!--enwiki derived wikidata interwikis--> [[de:Sōja (Shintō)]] [[fr:Sōja (shinto)]] [[ja:総社]] [[ko:소자 (신토)]] [[simple:Sōja shrine]] [[zh:總社]]

{{Other uses|Soja (disambiguation)}}
{{nihongo|'''Sōja'''|総社|}} is a type of [[Shinto shrine]] where the ''[[kami]]'' of a region are grouped together into a single sanctuary. This "region" may refer to a ''[[shōen]]'', village or geographic area, but is more generally referred to as a whole [[Provinces of Japan|province]]. The term is also occasionally called "'''sōsha'''". The ''sōja'' are usually located near the [[kokufu|provincial capital]] established in the [[Nara period]] under then ''[[ritsuryō]]'' system, and can either be a newly created shrine, or a designation for an existing shrine. The "sōja" can also be the "[[ichinomiya]]" of the province, which themselves are of great ritual importance.<ref name="Herbert">{{cite book |last1=Herbert |first1=Jean |title=Shinto:At the Fountain-head of Japan |date=2011 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-0-415-59348-9 |page=105}}</ref>
Whenever a new [[Kokushi (official)|kokushi]] was appointed by the central government to govern a province, it was necessary for him to visit all of the sanctuaries of his province in order to complete the rites necessary for ceremonial inauguration. Grouping the ''kami'' into one location near the capital of the province greatly facilitated this duty,<ref name="Bocking">{{cite book |last1=Bocking |first1=Brian |title=A Popular Dictionary of Shinto |date=2016 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1138979079}}</ref>
The first mention of "sōja" appeared in the [[:en:Heian period|Heian period]], in the diary of Taira no Tokinori, dated March 9, 1099 in reference to the province of [[:en:Inaba|Inaba]]. <ref name="Hardacre">{{cite book |last1=Hardacre |first1=Helen |title=Shinto: A History |date=2016 |publisher=Oxford University Press |isbn=978-0190621711}}</ref>
The name "Sōja" is also found in place names such as the city of [[Sōja]] in [[Okayama Prefecture]].
Rokusho shrine (six place) is a very common Soja shrine name<ref name="Hardacre" />.{{Rp|page=601}}
